<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into numerous applications across various domains, LLMs’ safety becomes a critical concern for both application developers and intended users.
Currently, great efforts have been made to develop safety benchmarks with fine-grained taxonomies.
However, these benchmarks’ taxonomies are disparate with different safety policies.
Thus, existing safeguards trained on these benchmarks are either coarse-grained to only distinguish between “safe” and “unsafe,” or constrained by the narrow risk taxonomies of a single benchmark.
To leverage these fine-grained safety taxonomies across multiple safety benchmarks, in this paper, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span>, a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">G</span>eneralizable <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">S</span>afety <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">P</span>olicy <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">R</span>easoner to identify unsafe input prompts and LLMs’ outputs with violated safety taxonomies through Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO).
Unlike prior safeguards which only cover a fixed set of risk factors, our <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> incentivizes its reasoning capability with varied safety taxonomies through our careful cold-start strategy and reward design.
Consequently, our <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> can be trained across multiple safety benchmarks with distinct taxonomies and naturally exhibits powerful generalization ability.
We conduct extensive experiments to show that our <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> significantly improves existing safety guardrails’ reasoning capabilities for both safety and category prediction tasks.
Moreover, our <span id="abstract1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> not only demonstrates powerful safety generalization abilities but also achieves the least inference token costs with explanations.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have showcased the remarkable context-understanding and tool-using capabilities and are currently widely used for a wide range of applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, due to the inherent vulnerabilities of language modeling, LLMs’ unrestricted access control and unpredictable responses worsen people’s safety and privacy concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jing et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Currently, the safety risks of LLMs primarily arise from their inputs and outputs.
For the input prompts, malicious user queries such as jailbreaking, red-teaming and prompt injection attempts, may mislead LLMs into generating inappropriate content.
For the output, even though these input prompts are benign, LLMs may still output unsafe responses.
For example, despite benign input prompts, in retrieval-augmented generation scenarios, LLMs could potentially be corrupted by the retrieved web data and generate harmful or toxic content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate these potential risks, a range of defensive mechanisms has been proposed to enhance LLMs’ internal safety, spanning from pre-training data cleaning to post-training safety alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wallace et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.
Beyond strengthening the models’ internal safety capabilities, external safeguard systems are also widely adopted.
These safeguards include naive neural network classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Markov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta, 2024</a>)</cite> and advanced LLM-based guardrails which leverage LLM-as-a-judge to determine potential safety issues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
To facilitate the training process of external safeguards, numerous safety benchmarks present diverse safety taxonomies to pinpoint the categories of potential safety risks.
These taxonomies enable safeguard systems for fine-grained evaluation in addition to the safe and unsafe binary classification.
Moreover, these fine-grained risk categories further incentivize a series of safety reasoner guardrails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to offer justifications for their safety decisions and achieve better performance.</p>
</div>
