<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Safety evaluation results on in-domain testing sets. S-Acc, S-F1, and C-Acc represent safety prediction accuracy, safety F1 score, and category prediction accuracy, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">In-domain Safety Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the testing splits of our training data to perform the in-domain safety evaluation.
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the overall performance over the 4 in-domain testing datasets.
Its results suggest the following empirical findings:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">1) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our GSPR leads to improved safety prediction performance.</span>
Compared with existing guardrails and closed-source APIs, our <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> outperforms these baselines to achieve the best S-Acc and S-F1 over in-domain testing datasets.
Specifically, our <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> improves the overall S-Acc from 84% to 86% with the cold-start strategy, while Gemini-2.5-Flash and o3-mini only obtain approximately 74% S-Acc.
In summary, our <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> appears to be an effective guardrail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">2) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Existing safety guardrail models commonly lack the ability to reason on fine-grained safety policies, while our GSPR brings non-parallel category prediction performance.</span>
Beyond the safety prediction, LlamaGuard3 and GuardReasoner demonstrate poor category prediction performance with nearly 0% C-Acc.
We manually investigate their generations and discover that they can only output in their fixed formats and fail to follow our instructions to identify the fine-grained policies.
On the other hand, our <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> attains substantial gains over the fine-grained category prediction.
Compared with RSafe and ShieldGemma, our <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> yields more than 45% and 7% overall C-Acc, respectively.
Such improvements suggest <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span>’s methodologies effectively enhance the model’s ability to accurately and reliably predict the applicable safety policies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Out-of-domain Safety Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further validate the generalization safety performance, we conduct an out-of-domain safety evaluation using 4 additional testing datasets with unseen safety policies.
The evaluation results are depicted in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Out-of-domain Safety Evaluation ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and imply the following suggestion:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">3) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our GSPR demonstrates robust and powerful generalization abilities of safety reasoning.</span>
For overall S-Acc and C-Acc, our <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> still achieves the best performance under the out-of-domain setting.
Moreover, compared with the Qwen2.5 base model, our <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> obtains 6% safety accuracy and 25% category accuracy on average.
These results validate that our proposed guardrail model has better generalizable safety reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">4) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fine-grained category prediction poses a greater challenge than the binary safety prediction, particularly in the out-of-domain scenarios.</span>
For both in-domain and out-of-domain setups, the overall C-Acc is generally around 10% lower than the overall S-Acc across all evaluated models, which suggests that safety category prediction is more difficult.
Notably, for the HEx-PHI test set, the best-performing model achieves only 58.28% C-Acc, while all models surpass 95% in S-Acc.
Such results indicate that there is still room to generalize the safety reasoning to the out-of-domain distributions to reduce the performance gap between S-Acc and C-Acc.</p>
</div>
