</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss and compare the results RL-aligned guardrails models on Tables <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Out-of-domain Safety Evaluation ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to perform ablation studies.
We first show the importance of prompt templates to incentivize fine-grained category reasoning and then validate the effectiveness of the cold-start SFT.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">How Fine-grained Category Reasoning is Incentivized?</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">“<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> (safety only)” uses the prompt template of <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> but is trained on the same rewards as RSafe without assigning any category reward.
However, as shown in Tables <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Out-of-domain Safety Evaluation ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> (safety only)” can still achieve 66% C-Acc for in-domain setup and 72% C-Acc for out-of-domain setup, which significantly surpasses RSafe’s category prediction performance.
Therefore, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span>’s prompting template plays a crucial role in empowering guardrail models’ category reasoning abilities by introducing flexible safety policies and instructing models to output applicable fine-grained categories.
What’s more, after the category rewards are assigned to “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o</span> Cold-start” and “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/</span> Cold-start”, their overall category accuracies can be further improved to approximately 78%.
These results highlight the importance of effective prompting in boosting category reasoning performance and demonstrate that additional category rewards can further amplify this improvement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effectiveness of the Cold-start Strategy</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After comparing “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/</span> Cold-start” with “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o</span> Cold-start” in Tables <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Out-of-domain Safety Evaluation ‣ 4.2 Experiments on Content Moderation ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we show that our cold-start strategy consistently improves the overall safety prediction and category prediction performance.
For example, under the Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct base model, the cold-start strategy brings more than 20% C-Acc gains and results in state-of-the-art category prediction performance.
These results validate the necessity of the cold-start SFT with carefully curated samples before subsequent RL-based alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Analyses on Responses</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct both quantitative and qualitative evaluations of the generated responses.
Our quantitative analysis focuses on three metrics: response length (for cost analysis), language switching between English and Chinese, and repetition.
Under the exact in-domain setting, we report the average number of words per response (Avg Word #), the proportion of responses exhibiting language-mixing (Mix %), and the proportion of responses containing more than 10 repeated 5-grams (Repeat %).
The results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Analyses on Responses ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and indicate the following findings:</p>
</div>
