<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safety Threats on LLMs</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs are inherently vulnerable to a series of safety attacks and may potentially leak sensitive information.
Jailbreak attacks leverage intentionally crafted prompts, such as role plays and adversarial red teaming, to bypass the model’s built-in safety mechanisms to generate restricted responses for malicious purposes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Prompt injection attacks insert their injection payloads into the prompts to override, alter, or hijack the original instructions such as system prompt extraction and goal hijacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez &amp; Ribeiro, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greshake et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Backdoor attacks typically perform data poisoning during the pre-training and alignment stages to let LLMs misbehave when the input triggers are activated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.
In addition, LLMs also suffer from information leakage attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song &amp; Raghunathan, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and side-channel attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Defenses on LLMs</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance LLM safety, several defense strategies are proposed.
Internal safety mechanisms aim to further train LLMs though SFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, preference alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, and attack-specific training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wallace et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to improve LLMs’ robustness against attacks.
On the other hand, external safeguard systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Markov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>)</cite> are widely proposed to perform content moderation on both input prompts and output responses.
Compared to internal safety mechanisms, external moderation tools do not require an additional safety alignment tax, which may compromise LLMs’ utility.
However, external moderation tools require extra model training and inference costs.</p>
</div>
