<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Data Descriptions</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Training Data</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Defenses on LLMs ‣ Appendix A Related Works ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> depicts the detailed statistics of our training data.
For each split, we randomly sample 3,000 safe samples and 3,000 unsafe samples to train the guardrail model.
If the dataset has fewer than 3,000 safe or unsafe samples, all its data will be used for training.
The training data description is summarized as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aegis</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosh et al., 2024</a>)</cite> provides a comprehensive benchmark for dialogue safety, which collects 26,000 annotated human-LLM interactions.
Its safety taxonomy considers 13 high-priority and 9 low-frequency risk categories.
Notably, a novel “Needs Caution” category label is assigned for ambiguous content.
Aegis collects human-written prompts from various sources, including red-teaming and jailbreaking datasets.
And the responses are generated by Mistral-7B-v0.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
To ensure quality, a team of 12 human annotators is involved to make Aegis a reliable and high-quality benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WildGuard</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite> collects 86,759 and 1,725 instances for training and testing, respectively.
WildGuard covers vanilla and adversarial prompts for both harmful and benign scenarios, resulting in 87% synthetic data and 11% human-LLM interactions.
Then, several LLMs are used for both refusal and compliance responses.
The label is given by GPT-4 and human annotators audit 500 samples with over 90% agreement scores for prompt safety.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OR-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a benchmark for evaluating over-refusal behaviors for LLMs.
Over-refusal happens when LLMs incorrectly reject harmless prompts that share similar traits with unsafe content.
OR-bench contains more than 80,000 synthetic benign prompts across ten safety categories with automatic data generation workflows.
In addition, OR-bench collects a hard subset of 1,319 safe prompts that current LLMs consistently reject and a Toxic subset with 6,55 genuinely harmful prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GUARDSET-X</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> considers principled grounding to standardize safety policies according to the real and domain-specific safety guidelines.
GUARDSET-X collects more than 100,000 data instances with diverse formats.
Compared with other safety benchmarks, GUARDSET-X exhibits a broad domain coverage with more than 150 policies and 400 risk categories across 8 domains.
However, the data curation process heavily relies on LLM annotations and augmentations without careful examination and filtering.
We select the HR and Education domains to train the guardrail model for better safety generalization abilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SafeRLHF</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a large-scale safety benchmark with more than 200,000 question-answer pairs.
SafeRLHF is annotated upon both harmlessness and helpfulness.
For the safety aspect, SafeRLHF identifies 19 distinct harm categories under 3 severity levels.
Both the prompts and responses are generated by open-weight Alpaca LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and validated with a human-in-the-loop process.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BeaverTails</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>)</cite> provides a comprehensive question-answer pairs dataset for training safety-aligned LLMs based on helpfulness and harmlessness.
BeaverTails covers two annotated subsets: one safety subset of 333,963 QA samples and another preference subset of 361,903 expert comparisons for helpfulness.
Derived from adversarial prompts and annotated through a sequential two-phase methodology, BeaverTails facilitates the robust training of content moderation for safety-aware reinforcement learning from human feedback.</p>
</div>
