</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Testing Data</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ B.1 Training Data ‣ Appendix B Data Descriptions ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> depicts the detailed statistics of the testing sets for evaluation.
For each split, we randomly sample 1,500 safe and 1,500 unsafe samples to test the safety performance.
For each set, if it has fewer than 1,500 safe or unsafe samples, all its data will be evaluated.
Since in-domain datasets have already been discussed in Section <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Training Data ‣ Appendix B Data Descriptions ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, for simplicity, we only introduce the out-of-domain testing data, which are shown below:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenAI Moderation</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Markov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> is constructed by OpenAI and specifically designed for training practical classifiers for content moderation.
This dataset proposes a fine-grained safety taxonomy, covering five core harmful categories, including sexual, hate, violence, harassment, and self-harm.
Moreover, OpenAI Moderation identifies three high-risk subcategories, with clear distinctions between contextual differences (e.g., fictional vs. real violence).
OpenAI Moderation test set comprises 1,680 multi-label samples, which combine human annotations with model-generated data.
The OpenAI Moderation test set serves as an important benchmark for content safety research.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HEx-PHI</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a compact yet carefully curated benchmark to evaluate the safety of LLMs. HEx-PHI focuses on robustness across multiple harmful content categories.
It is grounded in the usage policies of OpenAI and Meta, from which 11 harmfulness categories (such as hate speech, violence, self-harm, and misinformation) are derived.
Each category contains 30 instructions that may violate regulations or lead to harmful outputs.
In summary, HEx-PHI includes 330 instructions to offer systematic and diverse coverage for assessing LLMs’ safety performance under multi-category risk scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T2T</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi, 2024</a>)</cite> refers to the T2T Synthetic Safety Bench.
T2T is a synthetic question-answering dataset intended for content moderation for LLMs.
T2T contains approximately 2,500 examples, with safety labels divided into four types: unsafe, safe_related, safe_unrelated, and safe_unrelated_unsafe.
In our paper, the safe_unrelated_unsafe category is excluded to enable binary evaluation of user prompts (safe vs. unsafe) and subsequent fine-grained classification.
The dataset currently covers three risk categories, including “sex-related crimes,” “suicide and self-harm,” and “violent crimes.”
T2T is generated through a semi-automated process.
First, high-controllability LLMs are used to create initial question-answer pairs.
Then, low-controllability LLMs expand these pairs.
Lastly, all instances are manually reviewed to ensure both safety and diversity.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Do-Not-Answer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> provides a diverse and challenging benchmark for studying LLM refusal alignment.
Do-Not-Answer is a question-answer dataset crafted to evaluate the safety capabilities of LLMs.
It establishes a hierarchical classification system, which consists of 5 first-level risk domains, 12 second-level harm types, and 61 third-level specific risk types.
In our paper, we focus on the second-level types and its question set contains 939 risk instructions.
For each risk type, around 15 templates are generated via GPT-4 and supplemented with sensitive details.
Moreover, boundary cases (e.g., “How can I scare someone?”) are addressed accordingly.
The response set collects outputs from six mainstream LLMs.
In this paper, we use the ChatGLM2-7B subset.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>More on Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Baseline LLM Guardrails</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce more background knowledge of our evaluated baseline LLM guardrails, which are shown below:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ShieldGemma-9B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a comprehensive content moderation model trained on Gemma2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Based on a novel synthetic data generation, its training data encompasses high-quality, adversarial and diverse instances across 6 risk categories defined by principles of Google’s Responsible Generative AI Toolkit.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LlamaGuard3-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>)</cite> is developed by Meta and fine-tuned on Llama3-8B base model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI@Meta, 2024</a>)</cite>.
LlamaGuard3-8B is trained to identify 14 pre-defined safety policies.
LlamaGuard3-8B strictly enforces the output format to first output the safety label and then output the category label without further explanations.
Through our experiments, we manually implement a parser to capture the predictions.
However, LlamaGuard3-8B fails to correctly predict the fine-grained category label under our prompt format.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GuardReasoner-8B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> performs SFT and hard sample direct preference optimization (HS-DPO) on the Llama 3.2 base model.
For training data, GuardReasoner collects more than 127,000 training samples from diverse benchmarks and leverages GPT-4o to synthesize its reasoning processes to gather 460,000 detailed reasoning steps.
However, GuardReasoner is not explicitly trained to predict the fine-grained safety categories.
Even though GuardReasoner can generate its reasoning traces for our testing data, it fails to identify the correct category labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RSafe</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> considers 18 pre-defined safety policies and collects approximately 10,000 training samples.
Without any extra cold-starting, RSafe directly performs Group Relative Policy Optimization on its training data to reason on the safety label only.
In our experiments, we follow RSafe’s official training pipelines and use our training data to train RSafe on both Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen3-8B.
Compared with our <span id="A3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span>, RSafe only presents a naive format reward and a safety reward.
As shown in the results under Qwen3-8B, RSafe fails to generalize to other LLMs with language mixing and repetitions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Detailed Training and Inference Setups</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Configurations</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For GRPO alignment, we implement our code based on the VERL package <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> by using its GRPO advantage estimator.
To train our <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span>, we use 8 NVIDIA H800 graphics cards with a batch size of 128 and train the model using the Adam optimizer with a fixed learning rate of 1e-7 for 1 epoch.
To avoid the out of memory issue, we set mini_batch_size = 64, batch_size_per_gpu=8 and max_response_length=1024.
During rollouts, we set the number of rollouts to be 5 for each prompt sample with temperature = 0.7, top_p = 0.8, and repetition_penalty = 1.2.
For reward assignment, we set <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=0.55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=0.55</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}=0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}=0.45</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Performance</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#A4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Appendix D Case Studies ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we draw the training performance curves regarding the averaged rollout token numbers, correct format accuracies and safety prediction accuracies for both Qwen2.5 and Qwen3.
These results suggest that our <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> with cold-start SFT has the best performance with the least generated rollout tokens and improved safety accuracies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference Configurations</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During inference, we use vLLM inference engine by setting temperature = 0.0 and repetition_penalty = 1.2 to ensure reproducibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Prompt Templates</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we list the prompt templates we used to perform content moderation and cold-start data collection.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Content Moderation Templates</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For content moderation, we consider two tasks, including the safety of users’ input prompts and models’ output response safety.
The full prompts are shown in Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ C.4 Cold-start Details ‣ Appendix C More on Experimental Details ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cold-start Data Annotation Template</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To empower the per-policy reasoning and let the guardrail models better follow our desired formats, we leverage Gemini-2.5-Flash to distill its safety reasoning traces given the ground truth safety and category labels.
Without loss of generality, we show the cold-start prompt template for prompt safety moderation in Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ C.4 Cold-start Details ‣ Appendix C More on Experimental Details ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Cold-start Details</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the prompt templates shown in Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ C.4 Cold-start Details ‣ Appendix C More on Experimental Details ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we are able to collect Gemini-2.5-Flash’s responses through API calls.
For each response, we implement regular expressions to make sure its format follows our requirements and both safety and category labels are correctly predicted.
After the filtering, we keep 1,383 samples for supervised fine-tuning.
We use the Adam optimizer and set the learning rate to 1e-5 with warmup_steps_ratio = 0.1 and weight_decay = 0.01.
We set the batch size to 16 and run the training for 2 epochs.
Then, the tuned LLMs are used as the base models for the subsequent RL-based alignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T7" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A3.T7.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:428.8pt;vertical-align:-212.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-7.7pt,8.3pt) scale(0.962856957305488,0.962856957305488) ;">
