</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Case Studies</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct case studies to compare our proposed <span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> with other baseline models qualitatively.
Table <a href="#A4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix D Case Studies ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> presents one example for input prompt moderation, while Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix D Case Studies ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> gives one case for the output response moderation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the prompt moderation case in Table <a href="#A4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix D Case Studies ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, one adversarial prompt is given to elicit the justification to defend Nazis.
Both o3-mini and our <span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> correctly predict the safety and category label for content moderation.
However, RSafe exhibits conflicting reasoning traces.
As highlighted in the red texts, RSafe first mentions that “the query itself is not expressing any harmful
intent or spreading false information.”
Then, RSafe classifies the prompt into the “Political/Misinformation/Conspiracy” category and considers the prompt safe.
These conflicts in reasoning traces suggest that RSafe’s safety reasoning is still inadequate.
For the cold-start SFT, it reasons over each policy and justifies the prompt as “questioning a premise about morality” without expressing hate.
Even though cold-start SFT makes the wrong prediction, its reasoning traces are still consistent.
For our <span id="A4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> <span id="A4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/</span> Cold-start, it efficiently points out that the prompt “promotes hate towards a specific identity group” and then makes correct predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the output response moderation case in Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Appendix D Case Studies ‣ GSPR: Aligning LLM Safeguards as Generalizable Safety Policy Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, the response covers the methods to train a dog to be aggressive.
RSafe still demonstrates conflicts for its reason.
As shown in the blue text, RSafe admits the response can be controversial and may cause harm.
However, RSafe eventually considers the response as safe.
On the other hand, both cold-start SFT and <span id="A4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span> <span id="A4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/</span> Cold-start make the correct predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, both cases validate the inference-stage efficiency and effectiveness of our proposed <span id="A4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GSPR</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
